Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186240926 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}x}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\operatorname{cos}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e)-(\\operatorname{sin}0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186240928 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186240928 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{2x{e}^{-y}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{2x{e}^{-y}}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}(t-1)⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}2}+(\\operatorname{ln}2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\operatorname{ln}}^{2}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186240929 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186240929 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{P(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\underbrace{(-x)}_{Q(x,y)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ Notice that the domain of $\\mathbf{F}$ is ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256656 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9}\\right)(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =9{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9}\\right)(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\color{blue}{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256657 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256667 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256674 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256676 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256678 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256679 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i+x\\,j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256680 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=-2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256681 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256682 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=y\\,i+j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=x\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=y\\,i.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256683 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=j,\\,F(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256684 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=4\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-3\\,i\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-5\\,j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256686 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256689 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=4\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256690 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields." "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."] [:db/cas 17592186256697 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256701 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =-i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/92/92f46798ebeae9017c91ddae24e0f68cef81a1045314950bc4b56af003af0b42.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256705 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(0,-2),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,-2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(0)^{2}(-2)^{2}+1}\\,j=-4\\,i \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{(2)^{2}(0)^{2}+1}\\,j=4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1da33de6ab53eee30cb7dbdba61a437fd9e3209437d061d2c546386981962942.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256708 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\,i+(y-2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2(y-2x) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(y-2x)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(-2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1) & =-2\\cdot (1-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(1-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,3) & =-2\\cdot (3-2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(3-2\\cdot 1)\\,j=-2\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3) & =-2\\cdot (-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,i+(-3-2\\cdot (-2))\\,j=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/25/2595c11a1b30fee9d76028010d2c7e608250c0d48ce0191d7da2a5412af6a110.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256709 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,i+xy\\,j$ at the indicated points?" "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?"] [:db/cas 17592186256709 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),(1,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,i+xy\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(-1)(1)\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(1,2) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(1)(2)\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,i+(2)(-1)\\,j=-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png)" "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),(1,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(-1)(1)\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(1,2) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+(1)(2)\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,i+(2)(-1)\\,j=-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256710 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{2}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?"] [:db/cas 17592186256710 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-4,2) & =-2(-4)\\,i+{2}^{2}\\,j=8\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,1) & =-2(-1)\\,i+{2}^{1}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,0) & =-2(2)\\,i+{2}^{0}\\,j=-4\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png)" "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{2}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-4,2) & =-2(-4)\\,i+{2}^{2}\\,j=8\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,1) & =-2(-1)\\,i+{2}^{1}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,0) & =-2(2)\\,i+{2}^{0}\\,j=-4\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/58/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256712 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =-\\left(\\frac{-3}{1}\\right)i-|-3|\\,j=3\\,i-3\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{-1}\\right)i-|2|\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,2) & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)i-|4|\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png)" "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-|x|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =-\\left(\\frac{-3}{1}\\right)i-|-3|\\,j=3\\,i-3\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{-1}\\right)i-|2|\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,2) & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right)i-|4|\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186256712 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j$ at the indicated points?" "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-|x|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?"] [:db/cas 17592186259501 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t(2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t[(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259503 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-3\\,i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-(-3\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t(5\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =5\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t(5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259517 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i$ and $b=2\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,i+t[(2\\,i+2\\,j)-2\\,i] \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}] \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259518 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t\\,(b) \\\\  & =t(-i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =t(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259523 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-4i+j+5\\,k$ and $b=-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t[(-2\\,i+3\\,j+3\\,k)-(-4i+j+5\\,k)] \\\\  & =(-4i+j+5\\,k)+t(2i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j+(5-2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-4,1,5)$ to $(-2,3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t[(-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(-4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(5-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-4,y=2t+1,$ and $z=5-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{(2t+1)+(5-2t)}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2t-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{t-2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{t-2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259524 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k$ and $b=4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t[(4\\,i-\\,j+5\\,k)-(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i-2\\,j+4\\,k)+t(2\\,i+j+k) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(t+4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,-2,4)$ to $(4,-1,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t[(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+2,$ $y=t-2,$ and $z=t+4$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{yz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2t+2){e}^{(t-2)(t+4)} \\\\  & =(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+2t-8$, $\\text{d}u=(2t+2)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2){e}^{{t}^{2}+2t-8}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\int }_{-8}^{-5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{-8}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}({e}^{-5}-{e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{8}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}({e}^{3}-1)}{{e}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259552 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k,\\,K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields." "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{j}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."] [:db/cas 17592186259553 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-2\\,i,\\,H(x,y,z)=-\\,i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields." "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=-{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."] [:db/cas 17592186259579 :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ only." "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259584 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259584 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(4))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259585 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259585 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(1))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259586 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259586 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259587 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259587 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(1))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259588 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\operatorname{ln}2))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259589 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}({e}^{2}))-f(\\mathbf{r}(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259590 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259591 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(4))-f(\\mathbf{r}(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259592 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259593 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(1))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}1-\\operatorname{cos}0\\operatorname{cos}0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259594 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,t⟩,\\hspace{1em}t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(\\mathbf{r}(\\pi ))-f(\\mathbf{r}(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259602 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)(2\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259604 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{8\\operatorname{sin}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259605 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\hspace{0.222em}(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\hspace{0.222em}2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259606 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259619 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}t)(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259622 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(2-2)),\\,(0+t(2-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)+{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2t)}{2}\\right)+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4+\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2[4-(-1)+1] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(2+t(2-2)),\\hspace{0.222em}(0+t(2-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)+{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2t)}{2}\\right)+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\\pi t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\\pi t+4)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4+\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(4t-\\operatorname{cos}\\pi t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(4-\\operatorname{cos}\\pi )-(0-\\operatorname{cos}0)] \\\\  & =2[4-(-1)+1] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259624 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3,$ traversed from the point $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $(0,-\\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3,$ traversed from the point $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $(0,-\\sqrt{3}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186259624 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259628 :question/problem "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩.$*" "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}t+1⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186259628 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\hspace{0.222em}(1+t(2-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}t+1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t-1)+(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(3t-1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\cdot 3\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+5t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259645 :question/problem "![](images/58/58995f1bc5b45dbe0d30a9d0cbf704d792b955e7195c0e4dc3610ddf2e576579.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩.$*" "![](images/58/58995f1bc5b45dbe0d30a9d0cbf704d792b955e7195c0e4dc3610ddf2e576579.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2t-1⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186259645 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-1+t(1-(-1)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)^{2}-(2t-1) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-6t+1-2t+1 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-8t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\hspace{0.222em}(-1+t(1-(-1)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}2t-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3t-1)^{2}-(2t-1) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-6t+1-2t+1 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-8t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\cdot 2\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259646 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}t){e}^{1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259648 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186259648 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}t-3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259654 :question/problem "![](images/69/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/69/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186259654 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =9(1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259699 :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(i-(-i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(\\mathbf{i}-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259700 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259701 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259702 :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(4\\,i+2\\,j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})) \\\\  & =4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259703 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & ={x}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259704 :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(i+3\\,j-(-i+j)) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(2t-1)^{2}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t+1)+(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{t}^{2}+2\\,dt \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{4}{3}+10]-4[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+t(\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(2t-1)^{2}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t+1)+(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{t}^{2}+2\\,dt \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{4}{3}+10]-4[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259705 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=2\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259706 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259707 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259730 :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ only." "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259731 :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,xy\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected.  The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the coordinate axes.  Thus, the region is formed by four disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$ only." "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,xy\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected.  The region contains all the points of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ except those on the coordinate axes.  Thus, the region is formed by four disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259804 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3x\\,j-4y\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and let $C$ be a path in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259804 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259805 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=(x-3y)\\,i+2\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259805 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259806 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=4\\,i-(2y-3x)\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,3t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}3t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259806 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259807 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=-y,R=2z.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F={x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k.$" "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=-y,R=2z.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186259807 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186259813 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da7dc42ad4e9eb5b6df7c42f06d7a0f7767820693f8e99e073c7b8bd7e93441.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/9d/9da7dc42ad4e9eb5b6df7c42f06d7a0f7767820693f8e99e073c7b8bd7e93441.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259813 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e94a2c5d0faabff1beeadeccb6999aa2edd71fce06fff8a2655db74e585eba.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c54487081f2150216b7e41b1351f0f7b19c794b140297789f19d9afe830bd7b1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e94a2c5d0faabff1beeadeccb6999aa2edd71fce06fff8a2655db74e585eba.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c54487081f2150216b7e41b1351f0f7b19c794b140297789f19d9afe830bd7b1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259814 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab267c11b8457b0e7e1480ff5aabd3acaa15a07f54625c6d8103d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab267c11b8457b0e7e1480ff5aabd3acaa15a07f54625c6d8103d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259814 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/17/1764bfc51a25475162a70bad7239a36f5d099aa6157c71ed7ed3e02e474eb906.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/05/059731f0cac61f19382172192c6a9f7f366ecb758767797f9bc8311faa3865af.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each sta