g a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/e0/e07fca985a11d4edb66ea91d9a560ab134d81f5f20aaba8b376677d0de2dd3e7.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/78/78de6ce0133bec33c14eabb43db2e53eb8d76653aa1fc121c83f47c4b91c3b94.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259820 :question/problem "![](images/86/866a1812bd31e0443becf35a7cc2b8df1bc84a49091a94250b60fe3533e4b172.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/86/866a1812bd31e0443becf35a7cc2b8df1bc84a49091a94250b60fe3533e4b172.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259820 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4232577b0687d781f702a196ae2c0060d3264c1f44a45a404a0f9da03bc0dd1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7cfcef37ed6a52d5bd374fae2d7a00c4d8012e9ea8dbef43165c10bcc9471be.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4232577b0687d781f702a196ae2c0060d3264c1f44a45a404a0f9da03bc0dd1.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7cfcef37ed6a52d5bd374fae2d7a00c4d8012e9ea8dbef43165c10bcc9471be.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259844 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/51/51fafa71ff31592d9692348087cf0a20f6bbaa3b05e8519b4109d44207899a39.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/22/2287c33ff81973bcaef70917452c97282e0d0dd9ef06141d5beee8b53dec5836.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/51/51fafa71ff31592d9692348087cf0a20f6bbaa3b05e8519b4109d44207899a39.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/22/2287c33ff81973bcaef70917452c97282e0d0dd9ef06141d5beee8b53dec5836.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186259844 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9dd2fb4478ac443020e9cbebd64a2c9e4cdf5c7b6d5ce5cede854e0d7a3d804.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/b9/b9dd2fb4478ac443020e9cbebd64a2c9e4cdf5c7b6d5ce5cede854e0d7a3d804.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259845 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a423ab56220e438fb849d2025ab53b99bb74ef598e16110c911d691680130527.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/a4/a423ab56220e438fb849d2025ab53b99bb74ef598e16110c911d691680130527.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186259845 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works  against the particle for $x<0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x>0.$ \n\n![](images/cd/cd52e91c72d481980c9a48575da7ca3eaebd2ed27f96b1054cf5c1c5da1aa3d9.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a1/a1e8fa5262fc787b691b3753fc57ab39aceee7c57dbbe166c6da6f6effaf42dc.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf{F}$ works  against the particle for $x<0,$ while $\\mathbf{F}$  works with the particle for $x>0.$ \n\n![](images/cd/cd52e91c72d481980c9a48575da7ca3eaebd2ed27f96b1054cf5c1c5da1aa3d9.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a1/a1e8fa5262fc787b691b3753fc57ab39aceee7c57dbbe166c6da6f6effaf42dc.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186262619 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=5$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=5$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262619 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262620 :question/problem "Let $p=⟨6,-4,1⟩$ and $q=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨6,-4,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262620 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+(-4)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+(-4)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262661 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262661 :question/problem "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [:db/cas 17592186262687 :question/problem "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262687 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is positive” only."] [:db/cas 17592186262688 :question/problem "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/06/06c6dd24948a95d08850455d273c3456076850a47c980a086f06813b96bfd6ed.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262688 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is positive” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” only."] [:db/cas 17592186262690 :question/problem "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262690 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is true, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is positive” is false. Note that the flux $\\mathbf{F}$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero” only."] [:db/cas 17592186262691 :question/problem "![](images/45/453633f6309b9f1d2a3f7a8d4266ff10f571823cffd705d0bc93b930dce63d8f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/45/453633f6309b9f1d2a3f7a8d4266ff10f571823cffd705d0bc93b930dce63d8f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262691 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/84/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero” only." "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is negative” is false, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/84/844d5e5574571ae221a86f541820a4b215766451031f4eea678cee46a1b83e41.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero” is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/b8/b8b31d46646db2d88d8a3f5658ee876383795ad5ba9aa55eb9959d5f95c02dbe.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero”; “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero” only."] [:db/cas 17592186262697 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{x}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186262697 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262698 :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "![](images/4f/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186262698 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{j}.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262699 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186262699 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $\\mathbf{n}=-\\mathbf{i}.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262700 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=15xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{x}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186262700 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262701 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262701 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{x}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186262724 :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262724 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{1em}⟹\\hspace{1em}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262725 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" "![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262725 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/87/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{1em}⟹\\hspace{1em}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262726 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "![](images/fd/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [:db/cas 17592186262726 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262727 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "![](images/0b/0b652e9eae16ae78a06743e75513674cc6d7596aadf2bd07aca4398a99089348.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [:db/cas 17592186262727 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262729 :question/problem "![](images/66/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "![](images/66/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [:db/cas 17592186262729 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=\\mathbf{j},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262836 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(6i-j+4k)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(9i+j)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =4i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =6i+j+5k \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$" "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(9\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262845 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(4i+8j+2k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(5j+6k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))j+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$" "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262848 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(5i+j+7k)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(4i+2j)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i-9j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$" "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262849 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =x-w \\\\  & =(i+7j+3k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(6i+12j+8k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+4k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))i-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))j+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-5i+5j \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+12\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262863 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)i-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-12i+6j-12k \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262864 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262865 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3j+4k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =2i+k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-i+5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262919 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-\\operatorname{cos}y) &=0 \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{sin}y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(1,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186262919 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186262920 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }i-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f∇g+g∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}={y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})})=-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}=-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =x{y}^{2}(-2x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2y{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-2x{y}^{3}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}({y}^{2}(1-2{x}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2xy(1-{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262921 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\, &  & ∇f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{1}{xyz},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y,z)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{1}{xyz},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262921 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)∇h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,k)+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,i+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\,j+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\,k-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\,j-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i+2{z}^{2}\\,j+3yz\\,k-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-yz\\,k \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\,j+2yz\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =h(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xyz}({y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2xy{z}^{3}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{xyz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{{x}^{2}yz}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}}{xy{z}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-\\frac{y{z}^{2}}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265721 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265721 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is also true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265722 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265722 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186265723 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}x=0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265852 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265853 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,0)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf{i}-e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265855 :question/problem "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}y=-8x-4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}y=-8x-4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186265855 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{4}{x}\\right) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2xy+8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2xy+8)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{4}{x}\\right) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$"] [:db/cas 17592186265871 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(1,4)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265960 :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265961 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265962 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186266047 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186266050 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186266051 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186266052 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186266053 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186269008 :question/problem "Let $p=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix}$ and $q=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $p⊥q$, find the value of $n.$" "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$, find the value of $n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186269008 :question/worked-solution "We know that $p⊥q$ ($p$ and $q$ are perpendicular) if and only if $p\\cdot q=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that $\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}$ ($\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275236 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\color{blue}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275240 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t-4,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275244 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275256 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275256 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8ef08638551571caa43150080a8550a0b61ed24eaa7c6da73fe66edd161e8e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/28/28db5c349d3455c58f81da7c181586ba4e0ad5662f35faa2575d6622b3713988.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8ef08638551571caa43150080a8550a0b61ed24eaa7c6da73fe66edd161e8e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/28/28db5c349d3455c58f81da7c181586ba4e0ad5662f35faa2575d6622b3713988.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186275259 :question/problem "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275259 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/0e/0e8d0fcf619987f49869cc6ae9eb5cb0c7acbbb23f45ce8f64626dfd1debdc63.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2bec1f1da172b623f20d28fd0e9c5a248cf00dc621769851ca33b3b545b8592.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only." "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/0e/0e8d0fcf619987f49869cc6ae9eb5cb0c7acbbb23f45ce8f64626dfd1debdc63.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2bec1f1da172b623f20d28fd0e9c5a248cf00dc621769851ca33b3b545b8592.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186278287 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278287 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278288 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278288 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278291 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278291 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278292 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{v}\\right)-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{v}-\\frac{{e}^{u+v}}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u+v}(u-v)}{vu}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278293 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=4u+6v.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\,\\text{ }4\\, & \\,\\text{ }6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 6-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278296 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278296 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278297 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278297 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278298 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278298 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278300 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278300 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278391 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}+-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)^{2}-2uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-2yz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}+2{v}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278393 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xy+z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278393 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(xy+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =u.{u}^{2}+2v \\\\  & ={u}^{3}+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{3}+2v)\\sqrt{16{u}^{2}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278394 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278394 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\, \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{8}\\,{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =({u}^{2}\\cdot {u}^{2})\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & ={u}^{4}{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{8}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{5}{v}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278395 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}+3yz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278395 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-{x}^{2}+3yz)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-(u\\cdot u)+3\\cdot (u+v)\\cdot ({v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(-{u}^{2}+3(u+v){v}^{2})\\sqrt{8{v}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278411 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x+2y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2u+2v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =(u-v)+2(2u+2v)-(2v) \\\\  & =5u+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\cdot 6\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}(5u+v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{5{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6{[\\frac{5v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6[5+2] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278411 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2u+2v,\\,2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=6.$*" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+2y-z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=6.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278412 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*" "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6xyz\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278412 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =6xyz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =6xyz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =6(u)(u+v)(v) \\\\  & =6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}6({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}v+u{v}^{2})\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}v}{3}+\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{v}{3}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278425 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u-v,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =xy+z\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =xy+z\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=0} \\\\  & =(u+v)(u-v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(xy+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-{v}^{2}u]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{v}{3}-\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278427 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u+v,\\,2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =2xz\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =2xz\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y=u+v\\,z=2v} \\\\  & =2u(2v) \\\\  & =4uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2xz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\cdot 3\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =3\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278442 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u,\\,-3v,\\,4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-3,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-8,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2uv\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}-3v,\\hspace{0.222em}4v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-8,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x(y+z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u\\,y=-3v\\,z=4v} \\\\  & =2u(-3v+4v) \\\\  & =2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}x(y+z)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2uv\\cdot 10\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}4uv\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2}2u\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =5\\cdot [{u}^{2}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278443 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u-2v,\\,u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\,-3,\\,-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\,(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+2v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨6,\\hspace{0.222em}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}-6⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u-2v\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =(2u+v)^{2}-(u+2v)^{2} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le v\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\cdot 9\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{u}^{3}-3{v}^{2}u]}_{-1}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-6{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\cdot [2v-2{v}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278446 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,-uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278446 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-uv\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-4uv,\\,-2{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+(-2uv)\\cdot (-4uv)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+8{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨2x,\\hspace{0.222em}2y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-uv\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+(-2uv)\\cdot (-4uv)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+8{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278448 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,2,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩.$*" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278448 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\,u-v+{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\,u-v+{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(2+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+(u-v+{v}^{2})\\cdot (-2v)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\,x+z,\\,1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{u}^{2}v+2u{v}^{2}-2u{v}^{3}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-v+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=2\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨2+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v+{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(2+{v}^{2})\\cdot 0+(u-v+{v}^{2})\\cdot (-2v)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2uv+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{u}^{2}v+2u{v}^{2}-2u{v}^{3}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-v+2{v}^{2}-2{v}^{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278449 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨3u{v}^{2},\\,u-v,\\,2⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.$*" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨3u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le u\\le 0,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278449 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩|}_{x=3u{v}^{2}\\,y=u-v\\,z=2} \\\\  & =⟨{(3u{v}^{2})}^{2},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\,3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =9{u}^{2}{v}^{4}\\cdot 0+(3u{v}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-6uv-3{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,x+y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2uv+{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(u+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}u]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6(-2+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩|}_{x=3u{v}^{2}\\,y=u-v\\,z=2} \\\\  & =⟨{(3u{v}^{2})}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨9{u}^{2}{v}^{4},\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}+u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-6uv-3{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =9{u}^{2}{v}^{4}\\cdot 0+(3u{v}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-6uv-3{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-6(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2uv+{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(u+\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}u]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6(-2+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278450 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278450 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3,\\,uv,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\,-2{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-v)+uv\\cdot (-2{u}^{2})+v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3v-2{u}^{3}v+2uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-3uv-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{4}v+{u}^{2}v]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[-3{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278455 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\,v-u,\\,-2uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-2uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278455 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u-v,\\,0,\\,v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\,2u+2v,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-2uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2v \\\\ 1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2v \\\\ -1 & -2u\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩|}_{x=u-v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-2uv} \\\\  & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u-v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u⟩\\cdot ⟨2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)\\cdot (2u+2v)+0\\cdot (2u+2v)+(v-u)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}-u{v}^{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{3}v-\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278459 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 0$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}v-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $-1\\le u\\le 0$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278459 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\,u+v-(v-u),\\,-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-u,\\,2u,\\,v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\,x-y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =⟨-(u+v)-(-v),\\hspace{0.222em}u+v-(v-u),\\hspace{0.222em}-(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\  & =(-u)\\cdot 1+2u\\cdot 1+v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-u+2u+2v \\\\  & =u+2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-y,\\hspace{0.222em}-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u+2v)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[uv+{v}^{2}]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(u+1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}+u]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278461 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\,-1,\\,1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1-2u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278461 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1-2u,\\,2,\\,3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-4uv,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2,\\,-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v\\, \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & -2 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2uv & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & ={⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩|}_{x=u{v}^{2}\\,y=-1\\,z=1-2u} \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3(u{v}^{2})(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3u{v}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}-4uv,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\  & =(1-2u)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-4uv)+3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-3xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-8uv)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}uv\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278462 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨-u,\\,-u,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186278462 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\,{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=-u\\,y=-u\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =(-u)^{2}\\cdot (-v)^{2}-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}{v}^{2}-u)\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278463 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\,u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-y-z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2}-{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186278463 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\,0,\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-2u & 1 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2u \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2v)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(-x-y-z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =-u-({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})-u \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}-2u)\\sqrt{8}v\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278464 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨uv,\\,-u,\\,-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=x-{y}^{2}z$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}-u,\\hspace{0.222em}-{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186278464 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,0,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,2{v}^{2},\\,u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\,{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ v & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}v & 0 \\\\ u & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}v & -1 \\\\ u & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\hspace{0.222em}2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(2{v}^{2})^{2}+(u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(x-{y}^{2}z)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=uv\\,y=-u\\,z=-{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =uv-(-u)^{2}\\cdot -{v}^{2} \\\\  & =uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(uv+{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+4{v}^{4}+{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278470 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,{u}^{2}+2v,\\,{u}^{3}+3v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}+bu+c\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{3}+3v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}+bu+c\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186278470 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}-3,\\,2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2u,\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}+2v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}+3v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩{|}_{x=u+v\\,y={u}^{2}+2v\\,z={u}^{3}+3v} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩\\cdot ⟨6u-6{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}2-2u⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (6u-6{u}^{2})+0\\cdot (3{u}^{2}-3)+2\\cdot (2-2u) \\\\  & =6u-6{u}^{2}+4-4u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{2}+2u+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6)\\cdot {u}^{2}+2\\cdot u+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+2+4=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278471 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\,y+\\frac{1}{z},\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u{e}^{v},\\,u{e}^{-v},\\,u⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{e}^{v}+b{e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{e}^{v}+b{e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186278471 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\,{e}^{-v},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\,-u{e}^{-v},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\,y+\\frac{1}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\,u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\,u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\,u{e}^{v},\\,-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{e}^{-v}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-v} & 1 \\\\ -u{e}^{-v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & 1 \\\\ u{e}^{v} & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{v} & {e}^{-v} \\\\ u{e}^{v} & -u{e}^{-v}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨x-\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\frac{1}{z},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u{e}^{v}\\,y=u{e}^{-v}\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\cdot ⟨u{e}^{-v},\\hspace{0.222em}u{e}^{v},\\hspace{0.222em}-2u⟩ \\\\  & =(u{e}^{v}-\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{-v}+(u{e}^{-v}+\\frac{1}{u})\\cdot u{e}^{v}+u\\cdot (-2u) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}-{e}^{-v}+{u}^{2}+{e}^{v}-2{u}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{v}-{e}^{-v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot {e}^{v}+(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-v}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1-1=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278507 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,x⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,v,\\,u⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186278507 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,x⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x⟩{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=v\\,z=u} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2u⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot 0+{u}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}2{u}^{3}-{u}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281325 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281325 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281326 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281326 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281327 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281327 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281328 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}(u\\hspace{0.167em}v\\hspace{0.167em}w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281328 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix}+2(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281329 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281329 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281330 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281330 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281331 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281331 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281332 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281332 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281334 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281334 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186281335 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186281335 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281336 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186281336 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281337 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186281337 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2yz\\begin{vmatrix}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2yz\\begin{vmatrix}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281338 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281338 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$"] [:db/cas 17592186281339 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281339 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186281340 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281340 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\begin{vmatrix}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane." "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\begin{vmatrix}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186281341 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281341 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186281342 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281342 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186281348 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v{e}^{u} \\\\ u{e}^{v}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v{e}^{u} \\\\ u{e}^{v}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281352 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [:db/cas 17592186281352 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2v)-(2u)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2v-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-2u & <0 \\\\ 2v & <2u \\\\ v & <u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2v)-(2u)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2v-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-2u & <0 \\\\ 2v & <2u \\\\ v & <u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186281353 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [:db/cas 17592186281353 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -v \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3{u}^{2})\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-(1)\\cdot (-v) \\\\  & =v-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{u}^{2} & <0 \\\\ v & <{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$