V & ={∭}_{Δ}4{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4{w}^{2}}{3}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [\\frac{4{w}^{3}}{9}]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (12-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+3y,\\,v=-2x+z,\\,w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\,2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(-6)) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}4{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4{w}^{2}}{3}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [\\frac{4{w}^{3}}{9}]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (12-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374139 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given b