 & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(0-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /2}^{u=\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=\\pi }^{v=2\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1+0)\\cdot (-1+(-1))\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\pi \\le v\\le 2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(0-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /2}^{u=\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=\\pi }^{v=2\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1+0)\\cdot (-1+(-1))\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374152 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2}.\n$$" "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le xz\\le 2\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374152 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+{x}^{2},\\,w=xz.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 10,\\,1\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{3}^{10}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}du\\cdot {\\int }_{3}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{u=1/2}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=3}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (10-3)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+{x}^{2},\\,w=xz.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\le v\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{3}^{10}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}du\\cdot {\\int }_{3}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{u=1/2}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=3}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (10-3)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374159 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374159 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374166 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-5\\le x+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6}.\n$$" "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-5\\le x+z\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le y\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le y-z\\le 6\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374166 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-5\\le x+z\\le 5,,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+z,\\,v=y,\\,w=y-z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 10,\\,0\\le w\\le 6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{\\int }_{0}^{6}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{6}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-5}^{u=5}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=6} \\\\  & =(5-(-5))\\cdot (10-0)\\cdot (6-0) \\\\  & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-5\\le x+z\\le 5,,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+z,\\,v=y,\\,w=y-z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-5\\le u\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 10,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{\\int }_{0}^{6}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{6}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-5}^{u=5}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=6} \\\\  & =(5-(-5))\\cdot (10-0)\\cdot (6-0) \\\\  & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374173 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v={x}^{2}-y-z,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-2w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v={x}^{2}-y-z,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-2w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374173 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4}.\n$$" "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375103 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{\\left(\\frac{u-v}{2}\\right)}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{\\left(\\frac{u-v}{2}\\right)}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375123 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 2,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375123 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 6,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$ \n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v,w)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\le v\\le 6,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$ \n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}v\\operatorname{ln}u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\color{blue}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}u\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}w\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375335 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2x=2xy+2y-2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2x=2xy+2y-2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186375335 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x & =2xy+2y-2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(y-x-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x+1$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+2x-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-\\cancel{2(x+1)}+\\cancel{2(x+1)} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1-2x) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(-x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,0).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x & =2xy+2y-2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2y-2x-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{i}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2x-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ 2y-2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(y-x-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x+1$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+2x-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-\\cancel{2(x+1)}+\\cancel{2(x+1)} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1-2x) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(-x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186375495 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8\\left(\\frac{v-u}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v+u}{2}\\right)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8\\left(\\frac{v-u}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v+u}{2}\\right)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375502 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=3u-5v,\\,y=2v-u.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{3u-5v+2v-u}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le u\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=3u-5v,\\,y=2v-u.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{3u-5v+2v-u}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375502 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+5y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+5y$ and $v=x+3y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le 2x+5y\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le x+3y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+5y$ and $v=x+3y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as"] [:db/cas 17592186375509 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375516 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}dv \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 3,\\hspace{1em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}dv \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375516 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,|x+y|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.$" "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le x\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}|x+y|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186375804 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=i-j-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-i.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186376124 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186376124 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379335 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=8$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|=8$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379335 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379342 :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379342 :question/problem "Let $p=i+2j+xk$ and $q=3i+6j-3k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+x\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=3\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379360 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m & =|\\,m\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =12cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379360 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,m\\,|=12$ and the angle between vectors $m$ and $n$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $m$ onto $n.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m=$\n{{field-1}}" "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|=12$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{m}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{m}$ onto $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186379363 :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-3,2,4⟩$ and $s=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-3,2,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{s}=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{r}$ onto $\\mathbf{s}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186379363 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{|\\,s\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(1)+(2)(2)+(4)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+4-4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{s}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{s}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(1)+(2)(2)+(4)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+4-4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379366 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{v}\\,u & =\\frac{u\\cdot v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(1)+(1)(1)+(-2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+1-2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{v}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(1)+(1)(1)+(-2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+1-2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379366 :question/problem "Let $u=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $u$ onto $v.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{v}\\,u=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{u}$ onto $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{u}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186379369 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186379369 :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379931 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379931 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=10$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=10$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379938 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=2$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=2$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379938 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380062 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186380062 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380650 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=x\\,i+j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-2\\,i+y\\,j.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186382978 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382985 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}(xz+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}(xz+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x\\operatorname{cos}(xz+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382992 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{zz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186382999 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2xz+{y}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2xz+{y}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2xz+{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383006 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =(-2)\\,i-j=-2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i-j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i-(-1)\\,j=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =(-2)\\,i-j=-2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i-j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i-(-1)\\,j=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186383013 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={2}^{-2}\\,i+2\\cdot (1)\\,j=\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & ={2}^{0}\\,i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & ={2}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={2}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={2}^{-2}\\,i+2\\cdot (1)\\,j=\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & ={2}^{0}\\,i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & ={2}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=4\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/59/59c2db214327f1e078c0ddc5770e91db5e3de021b9e16fbf0ae7a29545a633d1.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186383083 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383090 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,i+({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1})j+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+z{y}^{z-1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{y}+{y}^{z}) \\\\  & ={y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}x+z{y}^{z-1})\\mathbf{j}+{y}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383104 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{x-y},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-3xy,\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i-3x\\,j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{x-y},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)={e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-3xy,\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383104 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h∇g+g∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3xy)({e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j)+{e}^{x-y}((2x-3y)\\,i-3x\\,j) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\,i-({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\,j)+((2x-3y){e}^{x-y}\\,i-3x{e}^{x-y}\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\,i-{e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\,i-({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h∇g+g∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)+g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3xy)({e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{x-y}((2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({x}^{2}-3xy){e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+((2x-3y){e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3x{e}^{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x-y}({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}(({x}^{2}-3xy+2x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({x}^{2}-3xy+3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383153 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(-1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-2)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-2,-1)}{‖∇f(-2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2\\,i-4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(-1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-2)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(-2,-1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(-2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383160 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8}\\,i+\\frac{2}{8}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8}\\,i+\\frac{2}{8}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(2,-2)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383223 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}yz-y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}z-x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+({x}^{3}y-2xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1) & =(3(1)^{2}(-2)(1)-(-2)(1)^{2})\\,i+((1)^{3}(1)-(1)(1)^{2})\\,j+((1)^{3}(-2)-2(1)(-2)(1))\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}yz-xy{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}yz-y{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}z-x{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+({x}^{3}y-2xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1) & =(3(1)^{2}(-2)(1)-(-2)(1)^{2})\\,i+((1)^{3}(1)-(1)(1)^{2})\\,j+((1)^{3}(-2)-2(1)(-2)(1))\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186383230 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)k \\\\  & =(6-2x)\\,i-8y\\,j+8z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =(6-2(2))\\,i-8(0)\\text{ }j+8(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+6x)k \\\\  & =(6-2x)\\,i-8y\\,j+8z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =(6-2(2))\\,i-8(0)\\text{ }j+8(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+8\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨2,0,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186383237 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}-2yz)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xz)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,-1) & =({e}^{2-1-1}-2(-1)(-1))\\text{ }i+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }j+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =({e}^{0}-2)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }j+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i+5\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5} \\\\ 2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-3.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}-2xyz)k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}-2yz)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xz)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}-2xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,-1) & =({e}^{2-1-1}-2(-1)(-1))\\text{ }i+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }j+({e}^{2-1-1}-2(2)(-1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =({e}^{0}-2)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }j+({e}^{0}+4)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i+5\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5} \\\\ 2-x=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383501 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u-5v \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u-5v \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383501 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u-5v$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{u+v} & \\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right)-(-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u-5v$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-5 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{u+v} & \\frac{1}{u+v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right)-(-5)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{u+v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383508 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186383508 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x={e}^{u+v}$ and $y={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }2u\\, & \\,\\text{ }2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x={e}^{u+v}$ and $y={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }2u\\, & \\,\\text{ }2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383515 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(u-v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186383515 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v))-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=2.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v))-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383547 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-1\\cdot u \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/42/427cb4b8e6497f75aecfe809aa084b0bf6f4ad167f3a0838aa40b1a50d115e1d.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-1\\cdot u \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/42/427cb4b8e6497f75aecfe809aa084b0bf6f4ad167f3a0838aa40b1a50d115e1d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186383547 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?"] [:db/cas 17592186383568 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u-v) \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}(u-v) \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186383575 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-3,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+{v}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-3,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+{v}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385331 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(z\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385344 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{yz}{xyz} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xz}{xyz} \\\\  & =xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xy}{xyz} \\\\  & =xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }i+xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }j+xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{yz}{xyz} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xz}{xyz} \\\\  & =xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xy}{xyz} \\\\  & =xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+xz(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+xy(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)(yz\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+xz\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385377 :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385408 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}v\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385408 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $$\n\\displaystyle x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(u/2)} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\,0\\, & \\,\\text{ }\\text{ }\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $$\n\\displaystyle x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(u/2)} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\,0\\, & \\,\\text{ }\\text{ }\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385501 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(x\\operatorname{cos}(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385508 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(5,4)}{‖∇f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(5,4)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{j}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385515 :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385522 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385536 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y+0-2x \\\\  & =2(2y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x+2y-0 \\\\  & =2(2x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=2(2y-x)\\,i+2(2x+y)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(2y-x)\\,i+2(2x+y)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y-x &=0 \\\\ 2x+y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=2y.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y)+y & =0 \\\\ 5y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,0).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y+0-2x \\\\  & =2(2y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x+2y-0 \\\\  & =2(2x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=2(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n2(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y-x &=0 \\\\ 2x+y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=2y.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y)+y & =0 \\\\ 5y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186385536 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$" "Given that $g(x,y)=4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186385543 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0+0-1-0-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+0-0-1-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}-0-0-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,k.$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1.$  Similarly, from the second and third equations, we obtain $y=1$ and $z=1.$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y,z)=0$ at the point $(1,1,1).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0+0-1-0-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+0-0-1-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}-0-0-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} & =0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1.$  Similarly, from the second and third equations, we obtain $y=1$ and $z=1.$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(1,1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186385543 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇g(x,y,z)=0.$" "Given that $g(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186385550 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{xy},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=2x\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385550 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j)+{e}^{xy}(2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j)+(2x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)+g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{xy}(2x\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(2x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385755 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186385755 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 2v-(-2v)\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =4uv+4uv \\\\  & =8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 2v-(-2v)\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =4uv+4uv \\\\  & =8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387215 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387256 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387263 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186387270 :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)" "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186387284 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-4x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?"] [:db/cas 17592186387284 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,3),(-1,-1),$ and $(3,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2x)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,3) & =(3-2(1))\\,i+(2(3)-4(1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-1) & =(-1-2(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(3,2) & =(2-2(3))\\,i+(2(2)-4(3))\\,j=-4\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png)" "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,3),(-1,-1),$ and $(3,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-4x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,3) & =(3-2(1))\\,i+(2(3)-4(1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-1) & =(-1-2(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(3,2) & =(2-2(3))\\,i+(2(2)-4(3))\\,j=-4\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186387291 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?"] [:db/cas 17592186387291 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)" "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186387367 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =t(\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =t(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387381 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})+t[(5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387470 :question/problem "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186387470 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387477 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}t+4\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387477 :question/problem "![](images/9b/9babf4c2ec631b3b872760261a46c3552cb93b93df90515ff0605c15043841ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/9b/9babf4c2ec631b3b872760261a46c3552cb93b93df90515ff0605c15043841ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186387484 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f532544febd248846eede65941f4617d325d0f91fec291d2679ba28abb2fb8fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/f5/f532544febd248846eede65941f4617d325d0f91fec291d2679ba28abb2fb8fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186387484 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387491 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d02a56e1f927ccf8bd76a6fcc46dec61259649176695912865dd050657ce7f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/4d/4d02a56e1f927ccf8bd76a6fcc46dec61259649176695912865dd050657ce7f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186387491 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387498 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1956c8291f1bcecd7d37082110861ed7265fa40f7f85cbc69221cfdc5bea2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/fd/fd1956c8291f1bcecd7d37082110861ed7265fa40f7f85cbc69221cfdc5bea2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186387498 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =12{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12 \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2} \\\\  & =12{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+12({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+12 \\\\  & =4({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+3)\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+3)\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387533 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387540 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387547 :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387629 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186387629 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=y,R={z}^{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F={x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k.$" "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=y,R={z}^{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186388083 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $2{x}^{2}=y+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i+j?$" "At which point on the curve $2{x}^{2}=y+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388083 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ (-i-4x\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ -1-4x & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =y+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8}).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{(}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ (-\\mathbf{i}-4x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ -1-4x & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =y+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186389181 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [:db/cas 17592186389181 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389188 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?"] [:db/cas 17592186389188 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389195 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [:db/cas 17592186389195 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389202 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?"] [:db/cas 17592186389202 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389209 :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?"] [:db/cas 17592186389209 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)" "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389216 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186389319 :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-2y\\,i-(2x-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186389326 :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =15x{y}^{2}-x, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)=5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =(15x{y}^{2}-x)\\,i-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =15x{y}^{2}-x, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)=5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y) & =P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(15x{y}^{2}-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186389339 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ over the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186389339 :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)" "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2x\\mathbf{i}-2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389346 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ over the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186389346 :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)" "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186395845 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,3).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(0,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186395845 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186398228 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2-3t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\color{blue}4-9t})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186398361 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398507 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,3y,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}3y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186398507 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3x,\\,3y,\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩ \\\\  & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)+3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)+{u}^{2}\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v)+{u}^{3} \\\\  & =-5{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,-5{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}3y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u\\operatorname{cos}v\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}v\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩ \\\\  & =3u\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v)+3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v)+{u}^{2}\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v)+{u}^{3} \\\\  & =-5{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-5{u}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186398534 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xz,\\,y,\\,xz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,uv,\\,v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩.$*" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨xz,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}xz⟩,\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩,\\hspace{1em}(u,v)\\in D⊂{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186398534 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=uv\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨uv,\\,uv,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨uv,\\,uv,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩ \\\\  & =uv\\cdot v+uv\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=uv\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv,\\hspace{0.222em}uv⟩\\cdot ⟨v,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩ \\\\  & =uv\\cdot v+uv\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186398947 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186398954 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186400373 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186400373 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)\\mathbf{i}-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})\\mathbf{j}=0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 &=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186400450 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186400450 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186401275 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401275 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$" "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186401282 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401282 :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane." "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186401302 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401336 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401336 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186401357 :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j?$" "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186401357 :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=-2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4xy+(-4xy)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}=(xy)^{2}.\n$$" "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=-2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4xy+(-4xy)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}=(xy)^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186403468 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186403475 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.\n$$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$" "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.\n$$ \n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186403475 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z,\n$$ find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z,\n$$ find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186403571 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186403571 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(10x-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{i}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(10x-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186405132 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1,\n$$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1,\n$$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩+t\\hspace{0.167em}⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186406771 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186406772 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$" "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186407846 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186407847 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186407848 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186407849 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$" "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186414460 :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186414461 :ques