$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $AB^(→)$?" "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $7$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186179958 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*" "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}\\boxed{?}dt\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186179969 :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*" "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}xy^{2}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186179972 :question/problem "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above." "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}4xyds,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186180017 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$" "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180278 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\sin \\left(\\frac{πy}{4}\\right)ds\n$$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$." "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}\\sin \\left(\\frac{πy}{4}\\right)ds\n$$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$."] [:db/cas 17592186198948 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x + y \\le 3, 0 \\le y - x \\le 2}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y$\nand\n$v = y - x$,\nthe integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy\n$$\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x + y \\le 3, 0 \\le y - x \\le 2}$.\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y$\nand\n$v = y - x$,\nthe integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_{D}(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy\n$$\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as"] [:db/cas 17592186558856 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186558865 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=1(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=1(36) \\\\ &=36\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get