 region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+(- \\frac{8}{3}))-((-8)+(\\frac{8}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186705597 :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient." "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [:db/cas 17592186705614 :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient." "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [:db/cas 17592186705616 :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ \\sin(- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient." "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(0,\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [:db/cas 17592186705618 :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient." "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [:db/cas 17592186705625 :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{3})&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3})&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient." "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [:db/cas 17592186105592 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179587 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t[(-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k})+t(-\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186259551 :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$" "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186401302 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}(z-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\color{blue}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{3}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}v\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}w\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060031 :question/worked-solution nil "Multiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=(-1)(10)+(0)(2)+(6)(-5)=-10-30=-40.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186060184 :question/worked-solution nil "Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero. Thus\n\n$$\nu\\cdot v=x^2+(-2)(2)=x^2-4=0.\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=\\pm2$. Since $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$."] [:db/cas 17592186060185 :question/worked-solution nil "Since $a\\perp b$, their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x=3$."] [:db/cas 17592186096969 :question/worked-solution nil "Divide the vector by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\widehat a=\\frac{a}{|a|}=\\frac15a.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac15|a|=1$, and the positive scalar multiple has the same direction as $a$."] [:db/cas 17592186097042 :question/worked-solution nil "The horizontal and vertical vectors are perpendicular, so\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n$$\n\nDividing by this magnitude gives the required unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}=\\frac15(a+b).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097043 :question/worked-solution nil "North-east and south-east directions are perpendicular. Hence\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{20^2+13^2}=\\sqrt{569}.\n$$\n\nThe unit vector in the direction of $a+b$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{\\sqrt{569}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097045 :question/worked-solution nil "A unit vector in the direction of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ is obtained by dividing by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{|\\overrightarrow{AB}|}=\\frac17\\overrightarrow{AB}.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac17(7)=1$."] [:db/cas 17592186097161 :question/worked-solution nil "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\langle4,\\frac32\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097177 :question/worked-solution nil "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. The vector moves four grid squares right and seven down, so\n\n$$\nc=\\left\\langle2,-\\frac72\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad |c|=\\sqrt{2^2+\\left(-\\frac72\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{65}}2.\n$$\n\nDividing by the magnitude gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{|c|}=\\frac{2c}{\\sqrt{65}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106038 :question/worked-solution nil "The scalar projections are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|d|},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac{d\\cdot c}{|c|}.\n$$\n\nHere $c\\cdot d=-2+2+4=4$, while $|c|=|d|=3$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c+\\operatorname{comp}_{c}d=\\frac43+\\frac43=\\frac83.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106110 :question/worked-solution nil "Use the scalar-projection formula:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=|c|\\cos\\theta=14\\cos60^\\circ=14\\left(\\frac12\\right)=7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106121 :question/worked-solution nil "The area of the parallelogram is $|a\\times b|$. Compute\n\n$$\na\\times b=\n\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\-2&-3&-1\\\\1&2&1\\end{vmatrix}\n=-i+j-k.\n$$\n\nHence the area is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106122 :question/worked-solution nil "Take two sides with common initial point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle2,1,2\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\langle0,1,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is $\\langle-1,-2,2\\rangle$, with magnitude $3$. A triangle has half the area of the parallelogram, so\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\frac12(3)=\\frac32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186106125 :question/worked-solution nil "Use $PQ$ as the base. The side vectors are\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle2,4,0\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{PR}=\\langle1,3,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe altitude from $R$ to the line $PQ$ is\n\n$$\nRH=\\frac{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}|}{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}|}\n=\\frac{|\\langle4,-2,2\\rangle|}{2\\sqrt5}\n=\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{2\\sqrt5}=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124940 :question/worked-solution nil "Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the other fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=\\frac{3(x-y)-(3x+2y)}{(x-y)^2}=-\\frac{5y}{(x-y)^2},\\\\\nf_y&=\\frac{2(x-y)+(3x+2y)}{(x-y)^2}=\\frac{5x}{(x-y)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $x\\ne y$,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=-\\frac5{(x-y)^2}(yi-xj).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186125073 :question/worked-solution nil "The product is $fg=x^2y^2\\ln x\\ln y$, defined for $x>0$ and $y>0$. Applying the product rule separately to each variable gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(fg)_x&=xy^2\\ln y(2\\ln x+1),\\\\\n(fg)_y&=x^2y\\ln x(2\\ln y+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\nabla(fg)=xy^2\\ln y(2\\ln x+1)i+x^2y\\ln x(2\\ln y+1)j.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129505 :question/worked-solution nil "Choose $u=xy$ and $v=y/x$. The boundary curves become $1\\le u\\le2$ and $1\\le v\\le2$. In the first quadrant this transformation is one-to-one, with inverse $x=\\sqrt{u/v}$ and $y=\\sqrt{uv}$. Its Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}y&x\\\\-y/x^2&1/x\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\frac{2y}{x}=2v.\n$$\n\nUsing the absolute inverse Jacobian,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_1^2\\int_1^2\\frac1{2v}\\,dv\\,du=\\frac12\\ln2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129506 :question/worked-solution nil "The given substitution turns the boundaries into $1\\le u\\le2$ and $1\\le v\\le4$. It is one-to-one in the first quadrant: $x=(v/u)^{1/3}$ and $y=u^{1/3}v^{2/3}$. The Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2y/x^3&1/x^2\\\\y&x\\end{vmatrix}\n=-\\frac{3y}{x^2}=-3u.\n$$\n\nArea uses its absolute inverse, so\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_1^2\\int_1^4\\frac1{3u}\\,dv\\,du=\\ln2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186129643 :question/worked-solution nil "Under $u=x-y$ and $v=y$, the parabola becomes $v=u^2$ and the line becomes $v=1$. They meet at $u=\\pm1$. The inverse is $x=u+v$, $y=v$, with Jacobian $1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{-1}^1\\int_{u^2}^1 1\\,dv\\,du\n=\\int_{-1}^1(1-u^2)\\,du=\\frac43.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186134081 :question/worked-solution nil "The arrows show the counter-clockwise arc from $(0,3)$ through the left and lower portions of the circle to $(3,0)$. With the supplied parametrization, its parameter runs from $\\pi/2$ to $2\\pi$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos t,\\qquad y=3\\sin t,\\qquad dx=-3\\sin t\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\int_Cx^2y\\,dx=-81\\int_{\\pi/2}^{2\\pi}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,dt.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152431 :question/worked-solution nil "The diagram shows an ellipse centered at the origin, with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIndeed, these coordinates satisfy $x^2/9+y^2/25=1$ and trace the entire ellipse. Swapping the radii would produce a horizontally stretched ellipse."] [:db/cas 17592186152795 :question/worked-solution nil "The dashed lines locate the center at $(2,1)$. The horizontal endpoints are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, while the vertical endpoints are $y=-1$ and $y=3$. Hence the radii are $4$ horizontally and $2$ vertically. Therefore\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+1,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186172275 :question/worked-solution nil "At the three indicated points, evaluate the components of $F(x,y)=\\langle x^2,-2y\\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2,1)&=\\langle4,-2\\rangle,\\\\\nF(2,3)&=\\langle4,-6\\rangle,\\\\\nF(0,-2)&=\\langle0,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matching diagram has a right-and-down arrow from each of the first two points and an upward arrow from $(0,-2)$, with the displayed component displacements."] [:db/cas 17592186172276 :question/worked-solution nil "Every arrow points downward. Its horizontal component points left for $x<0$, vanishes on the $y$-axis, and points right for $x>0$. These features match\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xi-j=\\langle x,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector fields would have the same direction everywhere, while $-xi+yj$ would point upward in the upper half-plane and inward horizontally."] [:db/cas 17592186172292 :question/worked-solution nil "Region I is a filled ellipse, so it is convex and therefore simply connected. Region II is the plane with the entire line $y=x$ removed; it splits into the two disjoint half-planes $y>x$ and $y<x$, so it is not connected and hence not simply connected. Region III is the full plane, which is convex and simply connected. Thus I and III only are simply connected."] [:db/cas 17592186179958 :question/worked-solution nil "Use $y=t$. Then $x=-t^2/16$, with $-8\\le t\\le4$, and\n\n$$\nds=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac t8\\right)^2+1}\\,dt=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^2+64}}8\\,dt.\n$$\n\nAlso $32xy=32(-t^2/16)t=-2t^3$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int_C32xy\\,ds=-\\frac14\\int_{-8}^{4}t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $t^3\\sqrt{t^2+64}$."] [:db/cas 17592186179964 :question/worked-solution nil "Differentiate the scalar function with respect to each coordinate:\n\n$$\nf_x=2x-y,\\qquad f_y=-x+6y-4.\n$$\n\nThus its gradient vector field is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=(2x-y)i+(6y-x-4)j.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179969 :question/worked-solution nil "With $y=t$, the curve is $r(t)=\\langle2t^3,t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Thus\n\n$$\nds=\\sqrt{(6t^2)^2+1}\\,dt=\\sqrt{36t^4+1}\\,dt,\n\\qquad xy^2=2t^5.\n$$\n\nConsequently\n\n$$\n\\int_Cxy^2\\,ds=2\\int_0^2t^5\\sqrt{36t^4+1}\\,dt.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179972 :question/worked-solution nil "Choose the smooth parametrization $x=t$, $y=t^2$, $0\\le t\\le\\sqrt2$. Then $ds=\\sqrt{1+4t^2}\\,dt$ and $4xy=4t^3$, so\n\n$$\n\\int_C4xy\\,ds=\\int_0^{\\sqrt2}4t^3\\sqrt{1+4t^2}\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1+4t^2$. The bounds become $1$ and $9$, and $4t^3dt=(u-1)du/8$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C4xy\\,ds&=\\frac18\\int_1^9(u-1)u^{1/2}\\,du\\\\\n&=\\frac18\\left[\\frac25u^{5/2}-\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_1^9\n=\\frac{149}{15}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186179982 :question/worked-solution nil "Parametrize the segment by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle1+t,3t,1-t\\rangle,\\qquad0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n\nThe speed is $|r'(t)|=\\sqrt{1+9+1}=\\sqrt{11}$, and $f(r(t))=2+3t$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,ds=\\sqrt{11}\\int_0^1(2+3t)\\,dt.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180017 :question/worked-solution nil "Along the segment from $A$ to $B$, both the field arrows and the direction of traversal point right. Their dot product is positive, so\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot dr>0.\n$$\n\nReversing the path changes the sign of a vector line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C}F\\cdot dr=-\\int_C F\\cdot dr<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore statement I only is true."] [:db/cas 17592186180083 :question/worked-solution nil "For the stream-function convention $F=\\psi_y i-\\psi_x j$, differentiate\n\n$$\n\\psi_y=2x\\cos y-e^y,\\qquad\\psi_x=2\\sin y.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(2x\\cos y-e^y)i-2\\sin y\\,j.\n$$\n\nIts divergence is $2\\cos y-2\\cos y=0$, as expected for a stream function."] [:db/cas 17592186180177 :question/worked-solution nil "Parametrize the segment by $x=1+t$, $y=2-t$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $\\sqrt2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C2xy\\,ds\n&=2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(1+t)(2-t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt2\\left[2t+\\frac{t^2}2-\\frac{t^3}3\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac{13\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180278 :question/worked-solution nil "Use $r(t)=\\langle1+t,2t,1+2t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}4\\right)\\,ds\n&=3\\int_0^1\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\,dt\\\\\n&=-\\frac6\\pi\\left[\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac6\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186180281 :question/worked-solution nil "The segment lies on $y=0$. Parametrize it by $x=1-2t$, $y=0$, $0\\le t\\le1$, giving $ds=2\\,dt$. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C(2x-3y)\\,ds=\\int_0^1(2-4t)\\,2\\,dt\n=\\left[4t-4t^2\\right]_0^1=0.\n$$\n\nThe positive and negative contributions cancel. The arc-length factor is positive even though $x$ decreases."] [:db/cas 17592186198948 :question/worked-solution nil "The substitution maps the region to $0\\le u\\le3$, $0\\le v\\le2$. Solving for the original coordinates gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}2,\\qquad y=\\frac{u+v}2,\n\\qquad\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|=\\frac12.\n$$\n\nThe integrand becomes $y^2-x^2=(y-x)(y+x)=uv$. Therefore the transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac12\\int_0^3\\int_0^2uv\\,dv\\,du.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186056544 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186056546] [:db/cas 17592186057564 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186057569] [:db/cas 17592186057570 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186057573] [:db/cas 17592186093788 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186093793] [:db/cas 17592186094225 